The [[2012 United States House of Representatives elections]] were held November 6, 2012, to elect representatives from all 435 [[List of United States congressional districts|congressional districts]] across each of the 50 [[U.S. state]]s. The six [[Delegate (United States Congress)|non-voting delegates]] from the [[District of Columbia]] and the inhabited [[Insular area|U.S. territories]] will also be elected. Numerous [[2012 United States elections|federal, state, and local elections]], including the [[2016 United States presidential election|2012 presidential election]] and the [[2012 United States Senate elections|2012 Senate elections]], were also held on this date.

== Election ratings ==
Several sites and individuals publish ratings of competitive seats. The seats listed below were considered competitive (not "safe" or "solid") by at least one of the rating groups. These ratings are based upon factors such as the strength of the incumbent (if the incumbent is running for re-election), the strength of the candidates, and the partisan history of the district (the [[Cook Partisan Voting Index]] is one example of this metric). Each rating describes the likelihood of a given outcome in the election.

Most election ratings use:
* '''Tossup''': no advantage
* '''Tilt''' (sometimes used): slight advantage
* '''Lean''': clear advantage
* '''Likely''': strong, but not certain advantage
* '''Safe''': outcome is nearly certain

